To make the circle element look more 3D in CSS, you can apply some additional styles that create depth and perspective. Here's an example:

```css
.circle {
  fill: radial-gradient(white, lightblue);
  filter: drop-shadow(15px 15px 15px gray) brightness(0.8); /* adds drop shadow and lowers brightness */
  transform: translateZ(10px); /* moves element forward by 10 pixels */
  transition: transform 0.3s ease-in-out;
  background: linear-gradient(to bottom, #fff, #ffcccc);
  box-shadow: 10px 10px 5px 0px rgba(0,0,0,0.75);
  border-radius: 70%;
}

/* Adds a subtle gradient to the z-axis for extra depth effect */
.circle::before {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  top: -5px;
  left: -5px;
  width: 110px;
  height: 110px;
  background: linear-gradient(to right, transparent, white 10%, white, white 90%, transparent);
  z-index: -1;
}

/* Applies the same styles as .grid, .xaxis, and .yaxis for consistency */
.grid, .xaxis, .yaxis {
  stroke: rgb(224, 64, 64);
  stroke-width: 1px;
  shape-rendering: crispEdges;
}
```

In this example, we added a `filter` property with a `brightness` value less than 1 to make the circle appear less bright and more recessed. We also added a `transform` property with a `translateZ` value to move the element forward, creating depth.

Additionally, we added a pseudo-element `::before` with a subtle gradient on the z-axis to give the impression of even greater depth. This is just a simple example, but you can experiment with different values for these styles to achieve the desired level of 3D effect.

