To make the circle element appear more 3D, you can add some perspective and depth to its CSS properties. Here's an updated version of the `.circle` style that incorporates these changes:

```css
.circle {
  fill: radial-gradient(white, lightblue);
  filter: drop-shadow(15px 15px 15px gray) brightness(0.8) contrast(1.2) blur(5px); /* Adding perspective effects */
  transform-style: preserve-3d; /* Required for rotating the circle */
  transform: translateZ(20px) rotateX(25deg); /* Adding depth and rotation */
  transition: transform 0.3s ease-in-out;
  background: linear-gradient(to bottom, #fff, #ffcccc);
  box-shadow: 10px 10px 5px 0px rgba(0,0,0,0.75);
  border-radius: 70%;
}
```

I added a `filter` property with the following values: `brightness`, `contrast`, and `blur`. These filters help create the illusion of depth by adding shadows, highlights, and softening edges. You can adjust the values of these filters to suit your preferences.

Additionally, I added a `transform-style` property set to `preserve-3d`, which is required if you want to apply 3D transformations like `rotateX()` or `rotateY()` to the circle. Finally, I added a `translateZ()` and `rotateX()` transformation to give the circle some depth and perspective. Again, feel free to adjust these values until you achieve the desired effect.

Note that using these perspective effects may increase the rendering time of the page, especially on older devices or browsers. Use them sparingly and test their performance in different environments.

