To further optimize efficiency for handling a large amount of text, you can consider using a sparse matrix representation instead of sets or cell arrays for storing the word counts. Here's how you can modify the code above to use sparse matrices:

```matlab
function uniqueWords = findUniqueWords(text1, text2)
    % Preprocess the text to convert it to lowercase and split into words
    text1 = lower(textscan(text1, '%S'));
    text2 = lower(textscan(text2, '%S'));
    words1 = strjoin(text1(:,1), ' ');
    words2 = strjoin(text2(:,1), ' ');

    % Convert the preprocessed text back to cell arrays
    words1 = strsplit(words1);
    words2 = strsplit(words2);

    % Count the occurrences of each word in both texts
    [count1, loc1] = histc(words1, {' '}, 'uniform');
    count2 = histc(words2, {' '}, 'uniform');

    % Combine the word counts into a single sparse matrix
    wordCounts = sparse([loc1(:) loc1(:)], [count1(:) count2(:)]);

    % Find the rows (i.e., words) that only appear in Text1
    uniqueRows = ~all(wordCounts(:,2) > 0, 2);

    % Convert the indices of the unique words back to their original locations
    uniqueLocations = loc1(uniqueRows);

    % Extract the unique words themselves
    uniqueWords = words1{uniqueLocations};
end

% Example usage
text1 = fileread('Text1.txt');
text2 = fileread('Text2.txt');
uniqueWords = findUniqueWords(text1, text2);
disp(uniqueWords);
```

This implementation uses `textscan` to efficiently read the preprocessed text data as numeric arrays, which can be more memory-efficient than using cell arrays. The word counts are then computed using `histc` with a uniform binning scheme. By combining the word counts into a single sparse matrix

