response:
To implement bubble sort in C++ for sorting an integer array in ascending order, you can follow these steps:

1. Define a function to perform bubble sort on the given array:

```cpp
void bubbleSort(int arr[], int n) {
    bool swapped = true; // flag to keep track of whether any elements were swapped
    int end = n - 1; // last index of unsorted part

    // loop until no more swaps are needed
    while (swapped) {
        swapped = false; // reset flag
        for (int i = 0; i < end; i++) {
            if (arr[i] > arr[i+1]) { // swap adjacent elements if they're in wrong order
                int temp = arr[i];
                arr[i] = arr[i+1];
                arr[i+1] = temp;
                swapped = true; // set flag if a swap was made
            }
        }
        end--; // decrease range of unsorted part
    }
}
```

2. To use this function to sort the integer array in the JSON object provided, you can define a wrapper function that parses the JSON input, extracts the required array, sorts it using the `bubbleSort()` function, and then returns the sorted array:

```cpp
std::vector<int> sortJSONArray(const std::string& jsonStr) {
    rapidjson::Document doc;
    doc.Parse(jsonStr.c_str());

    std::vector<int> arr(doc["array"].Size());
    for (size_t i = 0; i < doc["array"].Size(); ++i) {
        arr[i] = doc["array"][i].GetInt();
    }

    bubbleSort(arr.data(), arr.size());

    return arr;
}
```

Note that we're using the third-party library RapidJSON to parse the JSON string. This library is lightweight and has a low memory footprint.

