In MVC (Model-View-Controller) architecture, the model represents the application's data and business logic, while the view displays the user interface. The communication between the model and view is facilitated by changes in the model's attributes.

When a new attribute is added to the model, the view needs to be updated to display it. Here's how this process works:

1. Adding the new attribute: When a new attribute is added to the model, it is typically done through a class or struct. This new attribute is assigned a name and data type, and any necessary initialization code is added.

2. Notifying the view: To ensure that the view is aware of the new attribute, you can implement an observer pattern. This involves adding an `addObserver()` method to the model, which takes a view object as an argument. Whenever the model's state changes due to the addition of a new attribute, all registered views will be notified.

3. Updating the view: In the view's implementation of the `observeValueForKeyPath(_:ofObject:change:context:)` method, you can check whether the key path matches the newly added attribute. If it does, you can extract the value of the attribute and update the corresponding UI element(s).

