for (let I = 0; I < inputArray.length; i++) {
  const item = inputArray[i];

  // If randomization is not enabled, add the item at its original position.
  if (!item.randomize) {
    resultArray[item.position - 1] = item;

    // If randomization is enabled, randomly select a position for the item.
  } else {
    let randomPosition = Math.floor(Math.random() * resultArray.length);

    // Shift existing elements to make space for the new item.
    while (resultArray[randomPosition]) {
      randomPosition++;

      // Wrap around to the beginning of the array if we reach the end.
      if (randomPosition === resultArray.length) {
        randomPosition = 0;
      }
    }

    resultArray[randomPosition] = item;
  }
}
```

This code uses a loop counter `i` to iterate through the inputArray, and for each item it checks whether the `randomize` property is set to `false`. If so, it adds the item to the resultArray at its original position (calculated by subtracting 1 from the `position` property).

If the `randomize` property is `true`, the code generates a random position within the resultArray using `Math.random()` and `Math.floor()`. It then shifts any existing elements at that position (and potentially surrounding positions) until there's enough space to insert the new item. Finally, it adds the item to the shifted location.

