To automatically prefix a string with another string before passing it in as a regular expression, you can concatenate the prefix and the regexp pattern using the `^` anchor to ensure that the prefix is matched at the beginning of the string.

Here's an example implementation in JavaScript:

```js
function autoPrefix(prefix, regexp) {
  return new RegExp(`^${prefix}` + regexp);
}

const myRegexp = autoPrefix("my-", /prefix/g);
console.log(myRegexp.test("my-string")); // true
```

In this example, we define a function `autoPrefix` that takes a prefix string (`prefix`) and a regular expression (`regexp`) as arguments, and returns a new regular expression object with the prefix prefixed to the original regexp. The `^` anchor ensures that the prefix is matched at the start of the string.

We then create a new regular expression called `myRegexp` by passing our prefix and regexp to the `autoPrefix` function. We test it against a sample string "my-string" using the `test()` method, which returns `true`.

Note that when using this approach, make sure to escape any special characters present in the prefix string, so they do not interfere with the regexp syntax. This can be done by replacing any occurrence of a special character with its escaped equivalent using a regular expression replacement technique.

