To convert the command-line arguments passed through `argv` in C to a null-terminated string (C string or C-style string), you can follow these steps:

1. Allocate memory for the C string. The size of this string will be equal to the total length of all the command-line arguments concatenated together, plus one for the null terminator.

2. Traverse through the `argv` array and append each argument to the C string. Make sure to add a space between two adjacent arguments.

Here's the C code snippet that implements the above steps:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    // Calculate the total size required for the C string
    size_t total_size = 0;
    for (int I = 0; I < argc; i++) {
        total_size += strlen(argv[i]) + 1; // Add space after each argument
    }

    // Allocate memory for the C string
    char *command = malloc(total_size);

    // Initialize the pointer for writing to the C string
    char *ptr = command;

    // Concatenate all the command-line arguments into the C string
    for (int I = 0; I < argc; i++) {
        strcpy(ptr, argv[i]);
        ptr += strlen(argv[i]);
        if (i != argc - 1) { // Add space between arguments except last one
            *ptr++ = ' ';
        }
    }

    // Print the final C string
    printf("Command: %s\n", command);

    // Free the memory allocated for the C string
    free(command);

    return 0;
}
```

