Here's the R code to check whether all non-NA elements in a character vector can be converted to numeric:

```R
is_all_numeric <- function(x) {
  is.na(x) | !all(tryCatch(as.numeric(x), error=function(e) TRUE) == x, na.rm=TRUE)
}

# Example usage
my_char <- c("1", "2", "3", "four", "5")
if (is_all_numeric(my_char)) {
  my_numbers <- as.numeric(my_char, na.rm=TRUE)
  print(my_numbers)
} else {
  print("Not all elements can be converted to numeric.")
}
```

The `is_all_numeric()` function takes a single argument `x`, which is the character vector we want to check. It first checks for NA elements using `is.na()`. If any element is NA, then it immediately returns `TRUE`, indicating that not all elements can be converted to numeric.

If there are no NA elements, the function uses `tryCatch()` to attempt converting each element of `x` to numeric. The `error` argument of `tryCatch()` specifies a custom error handler function that always returns `TRUE` (indicating an error occurred). This allows us to catch errors caused by non-numeric elements without throwing an actual error.

Finally, the `all()` function is used to determine whether all elements successfully converted to numeric. The `na.rm=TRUE` argument tells `all()` to remove any NA values when checking for equality.

Putting it all together, the `is_all_numeric()` function returns `TRUE` only if all non-NA elements in `x` can be converted to numeric. The example usage shows how to call this function and handle the result.

